  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 152 AM Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...  Patchy dense fog will be possible late tonight and \nagain Tuesday night.  \n\nA weakening disturbance will pass to our west and north today as \nstrong mid/upper ridging holds over our region. Essentially it will \nbe dry today, with a few sprinkles possible. Essentially no \nmeasurable rainfall as it will remain NW of our region as the upper \nridging holds. A dry sub-cloud layer associated with the high \npressure extending SW across our region from the Mid-Atlantic will \naid in keeping it dry. Expect mainly cloudy skies this morning with \nclearing from the west this afternoon. Highs again in the 65-72 \nrange. \n\nFor the fog potential, it appears that as the high pressure slowly \nshifts east, a weak WAA pattern will begin tonight with a more mean \neasterly flow developing. The near surface dew points will be on the \nrise by around daybreak Tuesday and beyond. Saturation is likely to \noccur late tonight especially in areas of the eastern Piedmont and \nCoastal Plain. This region should have low stratus and patchy dense \nfog later tonight and early Tuesday. This potential will exist again \nWednesday morning with the expected unusually high dew points and \ntemperatures for early Nov. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... No big pattern change for the next 7-10 days, at \nleast noted now. Essentially very small chance of showers mainly \nThursday, and any showers will bring light QFP. Remaining very warm \nat least through Friday. \n\nTemperatures will run 10-15 degrees above climatological normal \nthrough Friday. Highs will consistently reach the mid 70s \nto lower 80s, with warm overnight lows generally in the mid 50s \nto lower 60s.\n\nThere will eventually be a pattern change, but given the lack of any \nverification of any rain in the past two weeks, it is hard to buy \ninto the models at 6-10 days out at the current time. I am not \nbuying any significant rainfall for the next 10 days attm.",
